<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R78002> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/hasContent> "In summary, existing initiatives focus primarily on either the organization of coarse-granular bibliographic metadata or fully automated indexing and searching of publications:\n\n**Organizing bibliographic metadata** such as title, author, journal, and citations adds relatively little value. Researchers are interested in answering research questions and in an overview of the state of research on a topic. This is not supported by coarse-granular metadata.\n\n**Fully automated knowledge extraction** of publications is highly error-prone, and while it can in principle support searches for publications relevant to a topic, it fails when it comes to specifically answer research questions and generating overviews of the state of research in a particular area. This is due to the limited accuracy and coverage (precision and recall) of the underlying automatic language processing methods, which achieve accuracy and coverage of only sometimes well below 50% for necessary extraction tasks (such as entity and relation recognition), and thus as a result do not provide sufficient quality for building a high-quality knowledge base (even for the foreseeable future) despite decades of research on the methods.\n\nWith ORKG, we address the weaknesses of existing approaches by combining e.g. automatic methods with manual curation via crowdsourcing. Examples like Wikipedia, OpenStreetMaps or WikiData have shown how promising such an approach can be. "^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
