<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R78004> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/hasContent> "One of the key challenges for sciences in the context of digitalization is how we can transform scholarly communication to leverage novel digital possibilities. Over the last centuries, the way we communicate scholarly results has hardly changed. Still, we produce textual essays, which we can now after the arrival of digital devices and the internet easier produce and distribute. However, the new means of digital technologies, such as semantic representation, dynamic content as well as data and knowledge integration are not yet exploited. Increasingly, we see the inadequacy of scholarly communication through symptoms such as the proliferation of publications, the reproducibility crisis, or the deteriorating quality of peer review. \n\nKnowledge Graphs as complex fabrics of structured and formally represented knowledge gained recently quite some traction in research and applications. Differently from other data management or knowledge representation paradigms, knowledge graphs follow a holistic information, data, and knowledge organization paradigm, where different formalization levels (unstructured, semi-structured, structured, axiomatic), aggregation levels as well as assertional and taxonomic information can be represented and managed in a unified manner. This has resulted in an increasing interest in knowledge graphs from the industrial side and spurred a vast amount of research wrt. knowledge graph reasoning, retrieval, completion, quality, or question answering on knowledge graphs. \n\nIn this SmartReview, we give an overview of the current state of knowledge graphs in scholarly communication. We start by introducing and comparing Scholarly Identifier Systems, which are the foundation for building scholarly knowledge graphs. Then we give an overview of Bibliographic Knowledge Graphs. Finally, we introduce some domain-specific knowledge graphs organizing information for a particular research field."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
