<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789578> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#Seq> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789578> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "authors list"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789579> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/description> "Coral reef classification using Landsat 8 involves analyzing the satellite's multispectral imagery, especially its visible and shortwave infrared bands, to differentiate coral reefs from surrounding marine environments. By utilizing spectral indices like the Normalized Difference Vegetation Index (NDVI) and other water-related indices, coral reefs, which have distinct spectral signatures, can be identified."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789579> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789579> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Coral reef classification usng Landsat 8"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789580> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/description> "Soft supervised classification is a method in remote sensing where the classification process assigns probabilities to each pixel in an image, indicating the likelihood of belonging to a particular class rather than a definitive assignment. Unlike traditional supervised classification, which assigns each pixel to a single class, soft classification provides a \"fuzzy\" result, offering a range of possibilities for each pixel. This approach is useful when dealing with mixed pixels or complex environments where land cover types are not clearly separable. Techniques like fuzzy logic or the Maximum Likelihood Classifier (MLC) with probability maps are commonly used in soft supervised classification."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789580> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789580> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> " Soft supervised classification"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789581> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789581> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> " Lizard Island, Australia"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789582> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/description> "Fuzzy Maximum Likelihood (FML) is a classification approach that combines fuzzy set theory with the Maximum Likelihood (ML) method. In FML, each pixel is assigned a membership value for each class based on its likelihood of belonging to that class, allowing for partial membership rather than a rigid, single-class assignment. This technique accounts for uncertainty and mixed pixels, where a pixel may represent more than one land cover type. FML is particularly useful in remote sensing for improving classification accuracy, especially in complex environments where pixel spectra might not clearly correspond to one class."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789582> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789582> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> " fuzzy maximum likelihood (FML)"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789583> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789583> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> " Fuzzy c-means"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789584> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/description> "A Subpixel Confusion Uncertainty Matrix is a tool used in remote sensing to evaluate classification accuracy at a subpixel level, particularly in situations where mixed pixels\u2014pixels that represent more than one land cover type\u2014exist. Unlike traditional confusion matrices, which operate on full pixel classifications, this matrix considers the uncertainty and degree of membership of each pixel to multiple classes. It quantifies misclassifications and uncertainties by incorporating the fractional contributions of each class to the pixel's overall composition, thus providing a more detailed assessment of classification performance, especially in environments where pixel spectra are not homogenous."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789584> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789584> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> " Subpixel confusion uncertainty matrix"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
