<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789593> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/description> "Semantic pre-classification of vegetation using Landsat time series data involves analyzing a sequence of satellite images over time to identify and classify vegetation types or cover changes. By utilizing the temporal variations in spectral reflectance captured by Landsat\u2019s multispectral bands (e.g., NDVI), patterns of vegetation growth, health, and phenology can be monitored. This approach helps in distinguishing between different vegetation classes (e.g., forests, grasslands, crops) by incorporating both spatial and temporal information."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789593> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789593> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Semantic pre-classification of vegetation using landsat time series data"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789594> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/description> "Automatic Linear Mixture Model (ALMM) is a method used in remote sensing to analyze mixed pixels, which occur when a pixel contains more than one land cover type. Unlike traditional classification that assigns a single class to each pixel, ALMM assumes that each pixel is a linear combination of endmember spectra (pure land cover types) weighted by their abundance in the pixel. The model automatically determines the endmembers and their proportions, providing more accurate results for complex scenes with mixed land cover. ALMM is particularly useful for applications like land cover mapping, vegetation analysis, and urban monitoring, where pixels often contain multiple materials."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789594> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789594> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Automatic Linear Mixture Model"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789595> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/description> "Semantic classification in remote sensing refers to the process of classifying satellite or aerial imagery based on the meaning or context of the land cover types, rather than just spectral or pixel-based characteristics. It goes beyond traditional pixel-based classification by considering the relationships and context between different land cover types, using additional information such as spatial patterns, object shapes, and textures."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789595> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789595> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> " Semantic classification"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789596> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/description> "A vegetation gradient refers to the spatial variation in vegetation types or communities across a landscape, often driven by environmental factors such as climate, soil, topography, and water availability. It represents a continuum of plant species or biomass distribution, where different vegetation types transition smoothly from one to another, such as from grasslands to forests or from wetland to upland areas. In remote sensing, vegetation gradients are often analyzed using vegetation indices like NDVI to track changes in vegetation density, health, and type over large areas, providing insights into ecological zones, biodiversity, and land-use changes."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
