<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789603> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/description> "Image sharpening is a technique used in image processing to enhance the details and edges of an image, making features more distinct. This process increases the contrast between neighboring pixels, emphasizing sharp boundaries and fine structures. Common methods for sharpening include applying filters like the High-pass filter or Laplacian filter, which highlight rapid intensity changes in the image. In remote sensing, image sharpening is often used to enhance spatial resolution, especially when merging high-resolution panchromatic data with lower-resolution multispectral images, such as in the case of Pansharpening, to improve feature visibility and accuracy in analysis."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789603> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789603> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Image Sharpening"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789604> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/description> "Sea Surface Correction refers to the process of adjusting remote sensing data to account for the specific characteristics and variations of the ocean or sea surface, which can affect the accuracy of satellite imagery. This correction typically involves removing or compensating for factors like surface reflectance, atmospheric interference, and water absorption. Techniques such as atmospheric correction, radiometric calibration, and the application of water indices (e.g., the Normalized Difference Water Index or NDWI) are used to enhance the clarity of water features, correct for glare or distortions, and ensure accurate monitoring of sea surface temperature, salinity, and other oceanographic parameters."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789604> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789604> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Sea Surface Correction"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789605> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/description> "Water Column Correction is a process used in remote sensing to account for the influence of the water column (the layer of water between the ocean surface and the seafloor) on satellite imagery, especially when analyzing water quality, bathymetry, or coastal ecosystems. The water column can absorb or scatter light, affecting the accuracy of spectral measurements. Correction techniques adjust for the absorption and scattering effects caused by particles, dissolved materials, and water depth, often using models or empirical algorithms."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789605> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789605> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Water Column Correction"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
