<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789612> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/description> "Spectral unmixing in hyperspectral data is a technique used to decompose mixed pixels into their constituent materials or endmembers, each with distinct spectral signatures. Hyperspectral images, which capture data across many narrow, contiguous spectral bands, often contain pixels representing a mixture of different materials, such as vegetation, soil, water, and built structures. Spectral unmixing algorithms, like Linear Spectral Unmixing (LSU) or Non-negative Matrix Factorization (NMF), analyze the spectral data to estimate the proportion of each endmember present in a given pixel."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789612> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789612> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Spectral unmixing in hyperspectral data"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789613> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789613> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> " Spectral Unmixing"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789614> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789614> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> " Oaxaca, Mexico"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789615> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789615> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> " Spectral unmixing"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789616> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/description> "Euclidean Minimum Distance (EMD) is a method used in classification and pattern recognition, particularly in remote sensing, to classify pixels based on their proximity to predefined class centroids in feature space. It calculates the Euclidean distance between a pixel\u2019s spectral signature (in multi-dimensional space) and the centroids of various classes (endmembers or class prototypes). The pixel is then assigned to the class whose centroid is closest, minimizing the distance. This method is simple and effective for classifying data when spectral signatures are distinct, but it may struggle in cases of mixed or overlapping classes."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789616> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789616> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> " Euclidean minimum distance"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
