<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789620> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/description> "Land cover mapping using hyperspectral data involves analyzing the detailed spectral information captured by hyperspectral sensors, which collect data across hundreds of narrow, contiguous spectral bands. These sensors provide much higher spectral resolution compared to traditional multispectral data, allowing for more precise differentiation between land cover types based on their unique spectral signatures. Hyperspectral data can be used to classify various land cover types, such as vegetation, water bodies, soil, and urban areas, by applying algorithms like supervised classification (e.g., Support Vector Machines, Random Forest) or unsupervised classification (e.g., k-means clustering)."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789620> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789620> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Land cover mapping using Hyperspectral data"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789621> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789621> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> " Beijing"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789622> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/description> "The Spectral Correlation Coefficient (SCC) is a metric used to measure the similarity between two spectral signatures. It quantifies the degree of correlation between the spectral reflectance values of two materials or regions across multiple spectral bands. The SCC value ranges from -1 to +1:\n\n+1 indicates perfect positive correlation, meaning the spectra are identical.\n0 indicates no correlation, meaning the spectra are unrelated.\n-1 indicates perfect negative correlation, meaning the spectra are inversely related."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789622> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789622> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> " spectral correlation coefficient (SCC)"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789623> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/description> "The Spectral Angle Cosine (SAC) is a metric used in remote sensing to measure the similarity between two spectral signatures based on the cosine of the angle between them in a high-dimensional space. It is derived from the concept of Spectral Angle Mapper (SAM), which compares the shape of the spectra without considering their magnitudes."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789623> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789623> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "  spectral angel cosine (SAC)"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789624> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/description> "Spectral Angle Cosine-Euclidean Distance (S_D) is a metric used to compare the similarity between two spectral signatures, commonly used in remote sensing for classification or spectral unmixing. It combines two different methods: the spectral angle and the Euclidean distance, to create a more robust measure of spectral similarity."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789624> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789624> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> " spectral angle cosine-Euclidean distance (S_D)"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789625> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/description> "The Chris Elvidge Dry Spectral Library is a collection of spectral signatures for various dry land surface types, developed by Dr. Chris Elvidge and colleagues. This library is primarily used in remote sensing applications to help identify and classify land cover types in dry or arid regions. It contains spectral data for different materials such as soils, vegetation, and bare ground, across multiple wavelengths typically from visible to shortwave infrared regions."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789625> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789625> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> " Chris Elvidge dry spectral library"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
