<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789640> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/description> "Endmember spectral refers to the pure spectral signatures of distinct materials or land cover types in hyperspectral or multispectral data. These signatures represent the idealized reflectance or radiance values for specific substances, such as water, vegetation, soil, or built structures, across various wavelengths. In spectral unmixing, endmembers are used as reference spectra to decompose mixed pixels, which contain multiple materials, into their constituent components. Identifying accurate endmembers is crucial for the success of unmixing algorithms, as they allow for the estimation of the proportion of each material in a pixel. These endmembers can be derived from field data, spectral libraries, or through automated methods like pixel purity index (PPI) or N-FINDR."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789640> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789640> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Spectral endmembers"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789641> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/description> "The Pearson correlation coefficient (often denoted as r) is a statistical measure that quantifies the strength and direction of the linear relationship between two variables. It ranges from -1 to +1, where:\n\n+1 indicates a perfect positive linear relationship,\n-1 indicates a perfect negative linear relationship, and\n0 indicates no linear relationship."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789641> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789641> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Pearson correlation constant"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789642> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/description> "Root Mean Square Distance (RMSD) is a measure used to quantify the differences between values predicted by a model or estimated from data and the actual observed values. It is commonly used in remote sensing to assess the accuracy of predictions or model results, such as comparing observed and predicted land cover classifications or satellite image simulations."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789642> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789642> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "root-mean-square distance"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789643> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/description> "Synthetic AVIRIS (Airborne Visible/Infrared Imaging Spectrometer) data refers to simulated hyperspectral data that mimics the characteristics of real AVIRIS data. AVIRIS is a high-resolution hyperspectral sensor capable of capturing data across a wide range of wavelengths, from the visible to the shortwave infrared. Synthetic AVIRIS data are generated using modeling techniques or based on known spectral libraries, and they provide a useful tool for testing and developing remote sensing algorithms, calibration techniques, and classification methods in a controlled environment."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
