<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789659> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Fusion of hyperpscetral data with panchromatic data"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789660> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789660> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> " Paris"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789661> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789661> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> " Rome"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789662> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789662> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> " Gram Schmidt mode 1"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789663> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/description> "The Gram-Schmidt Adaptive (GSA) is an advanced variation of the Gram-Schmidt process for image fusion. It adapts the original Gram-Schmidt algorithm to better preserve both spectral and spatial features of hyperspectral and panchromatic data. GSA improves fusion by adjusting the weight of spectral bands based on their relevance, enhancing the quality of the fused image."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789663> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789663> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> " Gram Schmidt Adaptive (GSA)"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789664> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789664> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> " Smoothing Filter-based Intensity Modulation (SFIM)"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789665> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/description> "This method uses a Laplacian pyramid approach, which decomposes the images into multiple frequency bands, to fuse hyperspectral and panchromatic images. A Modulation Transfer Function (MTF)-matched filter is applied to each band, ensuring that the fusion process enhances spatial resolution while preserving spectral information effectively."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789665> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789665> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> " Gaussian Laplacian Pyramid with MTF-matched filter (GLP-MTF)"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789666> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/description> "This technique involves applying a Gaussian filter in combination with a Modulation Transfer Function (MTF)-matched filter and high-pass modulation to enhance the spatial resolution of hyperspectral images. The high-pass modulation focuses on the high-frequency spatial components, improving image sharpness and spatial detail while retaining the spectral characteristics of the original hyperspectral data."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789666> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789666> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> " Gaussian MTF-matched filter with High Pass modulation (MTF-HPM)"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789667> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/description> "Quality without Reference (QNR) refers to methods or metrics used to assess the quality of an image or a processed signal without requiring any reference or ground truth data. In remote sensing and image processing, QNR techniques evaluate the effectiveness of image fusion, restoration, or enhancement by comparing various characteristics, such as sharpness, contrast, or detail preservation, directly within the image itself. These methods are valuable when reference images are unavailable, such as in real-time applications or when ground truth data is difficult to obtain."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789667> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789667> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> " Quality without No Reference (QNR)"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
