<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789671> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "X-ray Diffraction (XRD)"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789672> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/description> "X-ray Fluorescence (XRF) is a non-destructive analytical technique used to determine the elemental composition of materials. It works by irradiating a sample with high-energy X-rays, causing the atoms in the sample to emit secondary (fluorescent) X-rays. These emitted X-rays are characteristic of the elements present in the sample, allowing for precise identification and quantification of elements, often in solid, liquid, or powder form. XRF is widely used in fields like environmental monitoring, mining, geology, archaeology, and material science due to its rapid, accurate, and non-invasive nature. It is particularly useful for identifying heavy metals, minerals, and contaminants in a variety of samples."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789672> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789672> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "  X-ray Fluorescence (XRF)"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789673> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
