<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789717> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/description> "Spectral matching in hyperspectral data involves comparing the spectral signature of a pixel in a hyperspectral image with reference spectra to identify the material or land cover type present. Hyperspectral images capture a wide range of spectral bands, providing detailed spectral information for each pixel. Spectral matching techniques, such as Spectral Angle Mapper (SAM), Euclidean distance, or Spectral Correlation, are used to compute the similarity between the pixel\u2019s spectrum and reference spectra from a spectral library."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789717> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789717> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Spectral matching in Hyperpspectral data"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789718> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/description> "Spectral matching is a technique used in remote sensing to compare the spectral signatures of unknown pixels in satellite or airborne images with known reference spectra (such as those from a spectral library) to identify materials or land cover types. It is often employed in hyperspectral imaging, where each pixel contains a detailed spectrum across numerous narrow bands."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789718> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789718> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> " Spectral matching"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789719> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789719> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Lethbridge, Canada"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789720> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/description> "Z-score distance is a statistical method used to quantify the difference between a data point and the mean of a dataset, expressed in terms of standard deviations. In the context of remote sensing or hyperspectral data, the Z-score distance measures how far a specific pixel's spectral value is from the mean spectral value of a reference class, normalized by the standard deviation of that class. It helps in identifying outliers or unusual pixels that significantly differ from typical class characteristics."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789720> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789720> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> " Z-score distance"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
