<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789798> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/description> "emporal unmixing is a technique used in remote sensing to analyze and separate mixed pixels in time-series data. This method decomposes the observed pixel spectra into distinct components or endmembers over time, which represent different land cover types or material compositions. Temporal unmixing is particularly useful when dealing with dynamic environments, where the composition of a pixel changes over time, such as in vegetation growth cycles, urban development, or land-use changes."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789798> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789798> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "  Temporal unmixing"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789799> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789799> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> " Hawaii Volcanoes National Park"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789800> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789800> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> " Multiple Endmember Spectral Mixture Analysis (MESMA)"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789801> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/description> "Change detection in hyperspectral images involves identifying and analyzing differences in spectral information over time to detect alterations in the environment. This technique is useful for monitoring land use changes, vegetation health, urban development, or environmental impacts. Hyperspectral images, with their high spectral resolution, allow for the detection of subtle changes in material composition, even when there are small spectral variations between different periods."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789801> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789801> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Change detection in hyperspectral images"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789802> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789802> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> " Change detection"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789803> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789803> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> " Umatilla County, Oregon, United States"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789804> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/description> "tripe removal is a preprocessing technique used to eliminate unwanted artifacts, such as stripes or bands, from remote sensing images, particularly in hyperspectral or satellite imagery. Stripes can occur due to sensor calibration issues, atmospheric interference, or data acquisition errors, leading to inconsistencies in the image. Various methods, such as wavelet transform, filtering techniques, and principal component analysis (PCA), are employed to correct these artifacts."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789804> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789804> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> " Stripe removal"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789805> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/description> "Sequential Spectral Change Vector Analysis (S\u00B2CVA) is an advanced method for detecting and analyzing spectral changes in time-series remote sensing data, particularly hyperspectral images. It extends traditional Change Vector Analysis (CVA) by considering changes across multiple time points in a sequential manner. S\u00B2CVA identifies and quantifies changes in the spectral signature of a scene over time, helping to track dynamic phenomena like land cover changes, vegetation growth, or environmental disturbances."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789805> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789805> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> " Sequential Spectral Change Vector Analysis (S 2 CVA)"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
