<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789841> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/description> "Soil salinity mapping using hyperspectral imagery involves analyzing the spectral data captured by hyperspectral sensors to detect and monitor salt concentrations in soil. Hyperspectral data provides detailed spectral signatures across a wide range of wavelengths, particularly in the shortwave infrared region, which is sensitive to the presence of salts and moisture. By identifying specific absorption features related to salinity, it is possible to map areas with high salt concentrations, which can affect crop growth and soil health. This method offers a non-invasive and efficient way to monitor soil salinity over large areas, aiding in agricultural management, irrigation planning, and land reclamation efforts."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789841> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789841> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Soil Salinity mapping using hyperspectral image"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789842> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/description> "Soil salinity mapping involves using remote sensing techniques, including hyperspectral and multispectral imaging, to detect and monitor the levels of salt in soil. High soil salinity can adversely affect agriculture and the environment, making it important to identify and manage areas with elevated salinity. Remote sensing data can capture spectral signatures that are sensitive to the presence of salt, particularly in the shortwave infrared region."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789842> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789842> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> " Soil Salinity mapping"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789843> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789843> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> " Bajo Guadalquivir, Spain"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789844> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/description> "Soil Salinity Indices (SSI) are numerical values or formulas used to quantify and assess soil salinity levels based on remote sensing data or in situ measurements. These indices are derived from the spectral reflectance properties of soil, particularly in the visible, near-infrared, and shortwave infrared regions, which are sensitive to the presence of salts. Common SSI methods utilize spectral bands that correlate with salt-related absorption features, allowing for the detection and mapping of salinity in large areas."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789844> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789844> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> " Soil Salinity Indices (SSI)"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789845> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/description> "Support Vector Regression (SVR) is a machine learning technique used for regression tasks, where the goal is to predict continuous values. It is based on the Support Vector Machine (SVM) framework, which finds the best hyperplane that maximizes the margin between data points in a higher-dimensional feature space. In SVR, the algorithm attempts to fit a function within a specified margin of tolerance (epsilon) while minimizing the error outside this margin."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789845> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789845> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> " Support Vector Regression (SVR)"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789846> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/description> "Sequential Forward Selection (SFS) is a feature selection technique used in machine learning and pattern recognition to improve model performance by selecting the most relevant features. It works by iteratively adding features to a model, starting with the best individual feature and progressively adding the next feature that maximizes the model\u2019s performance. At each step, the algorithm evaluates the inclusion of a new feature based on a chosen performance metric, such as accuracy or classification error. SFS is particularly useful in reducing the dimensionality of large datasets, improving computational efficiency, and preventing overfitting by selecting a subset of the most informative features."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789846> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789846> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> " Sequential Forward Selection (SFS)"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789847> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/description> "Electrical conductivity (EC) refers to the ability of a material, such as soil or water, to conduct electricity. In soils, EC is influenced by the concentration of soluble salts and moisture content, making it an important indicator of soil salinity and overall soil health. High electrical conductivity typically indicates higher levels of salts in the soil, which can negatively impact plant growth. In remote sensing, EC can be inferred using sensors that detect changes in electromagnetic properties, or it can be directly measured in the field using specialized instruments like soil conductivity meters. EC mapping helps in precision agriculture, land management, and environmental monitoring."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789847> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789847> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> " Electrical Conductivity (EC)"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
