<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789984> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/description> "Synthetic hyperspectral image generation refers to the creation of artificial hyperspectral data that simulates real-world conditions for various applications such as model training, algorithm testing, and environmental monitoring. This is achieved using computational models, physical simulations, or machine learning techniques to generate spectral data across a wide range of wavelengths, mimicking the spectral reflectance patterns of different materials (e.g., vegetation, soil, water, or urban features)."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789984> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789984> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Synthtetic Hyperspectral image generation"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789985> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/description> "Synthetic data generation refers to the process of creating artificial data that mimics real-world data for use in various applications such as model training, testing, and validation. This data is generated through algorithms or simulations rather than being collected from actual observations. In remote sensing and machine learning, synthetic data can be created to represent different land cover types, environmental conditions, or sensor outputs, particularly when real data is scarce, expensive, or difficult to obtain."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R789985> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
