<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R790088> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/description> "Linear co-polarized backscatter coefficients refer to the radar backscatter measured when the transmitted and received radar signals have the same polarization. In Sentinel-1 SAR data, co-polarization occurs when both the transmission and reception of the radar signal are either both vertical (VV) or both horizontal (HH)."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R790088> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R790088> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> " linear co-polarized backscatter coefficients"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R790089> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/description> "Cross-polarized backscatter coefficients refer to the radar backscatter measured when the transmitted and received polarizations are different. In the context of Sentinel-1's Synthetic Aperture Radar (SAR), cross-polarization occurs when the radar emits a signal in one polarization (e.g., vertical) and receives the signal in a different polarization (e.g., horizontal). These coefficients are typically represented as \u03C3\u2070 VH (vertical transmit, horizontal receive) or \u03C3\u2070 HV (horizontal transmit, vertical receive). Cross-polarized backscatter coefficients can provide valuable information for land cover classification and can highlight specific surface characteristics, such as vegetation types, soil moisture, or surface roughness, because the way these surfaces interact with different polarizations varies."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R790089> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R790089> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> " cross co-polarized backscatter coefficients"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R790090> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R790090> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Neural network based crop classification"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R790091> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R790091> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> " Kyiv, Ukraine"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R790092> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R790092> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> " recurrent neural network (RNN)"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R790093> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R790093> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Built up area mapping suing sentinel 2"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R790094> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/description> "Urban area mapping involves using remote sensing data, such as satellite imagery from Sentinel-1 (SAR) and Sentinel-2 (optical), to identify and delineate built-up areas in a region. This process typically includes data preprocessing (e.g., atmospheric correction for Sentinel-2 or speckle filtering for Sentinel-1), feature extraction (such as using NDVI for vegetation and backscatter coefficients for urban structures), and classification (using machine learning or deep learning algorithms like Random Forest or U-Net). Sentinel-1's radar data is useful for detecting urban areas even in cloudy conditions, while Sentinel-2's high-resolution optical data helps to precisely identify urban features like roads, buildings, and infrastructure. Urban area mapping is vital for urban planning, monitoring urban sprawl, and managing infrastructure development."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R790094> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R790094> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> " Urban area mapping"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R790095> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/description> "U-Net is a deep learning architecture specifically designed for semantic segmentation tasks, where the goal is to classify each pixel in an image. It is particularly effective for applications like medical image segmentation and remote sensing, such as land cover classification. The U-Net architecture consists of two main parts: an encoder (contracting path) and a decoder (expansive path)."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R790095> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R790095> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> " U-Net architecture"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R790096> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
