<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R790163> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Sentinel-2 Imaging for High-Resolution Urban and Agricultural Mapping: Insights from Sugarcane to Urban Growth"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R790164> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#_1> <http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R108103> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R790164> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#Seq> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R790164> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "authors list"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R790165> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/description> "Optical and Synthetic Aperture Radar (SAR) data are both valuable for crop classification, each offering unique advantages. Optical data, such as that from Sentinel-2, provides high-resolution multispectral information, including vegetation indices like NDVI, which are effective in distinguishing different crop types based on their spectral signatures. However, optical data can be cloud-affected, limiting its use in certain conditions. SAR data, such as from Sentinel-1, offers all-weather, day-and-night imaging capabilities, providing detailed backscatter information sensitive to surface roughness, moisture content, and vegetation structure."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R790165> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R790165> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Optical and SAR data for crop classification"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R790166> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R790166> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> " Chongming Island, Shanghai"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R790167> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/description> "Backscattering coefficient extraction refers to the process of obtaining the backscatter intensity of a surface from radar remote sensing data, such as from Sentinel-1 Synthetic Aperture Radar (SAR) images. The backscattering coefficient (\u03C3\u2070, also known as the radar cross section) quantifies how much of the radar signal is reflected back to the sensor. It is a critical parameter for characterizing surface properties and is widely used in land cover classification, environmental monitoring, and change detection."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R790167> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R790167> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> " backscattering coefficient extraction"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R790168> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R790168> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "R andom forest"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R790169> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/description> "GLCM (Gray-Level Co-occurrence Matrix) is a statistical method used in texture analysis of images, particularly in remote sensing and medical imaging. It quantifies the spatial relationship between pixels in an image, capturing the texture of surfaces by analyzing the frequency with which pairs of pixel with specific values and in a specified spatial relationship occur in an image. GLCM is based on the idea that textures in an image can be described by the spatial arrangement of pixel intensities.\n\nKey Steps in GLCM:\nMatrix Construction: A GLCM is created by counting how often pairs of pixel with specific gray levels occur in a defined spatial relationship (e.g., horizontal, vertical, diagonal) at a given distance.\nTexture Features: From the GLCM, various statistical features can be extracted to describe the texture, including:\nContrast: Measures the intensity contrast between a pixel and its neighbor.\nCorrelation: Describes how correlated a pixel is with its neighbor.\nEnergy: Reflects the uniformity or smoothness of the texture.\nHomogeneity: Indicates how uniform the texture is, with higher values meaning less variation."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R790169> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R790169> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> " GLCM (Texture analysis)"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
