<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R796198> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/description> "The PushShift.io Reddit training dataset is a resource for social media data collection, analysis, and archiving, specifically focusing on Reddit data. \u200B It includes around 5B tokens and is designed to aid research into social media dynamics by providing a comprehensive collection of Reddit posts and comments. \u200B This dataset captures a wide range of discussions and interactions from the Reddit platform, making it valuable for training language models that need to understand and generate social media content. \u200B"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R796198> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R796198> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "PushShift.io Reddit"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R796199> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/description> "The OPT small language model, released in May 2022, includes parameter sizes from 125 million to 5.7 billion, and is pre-trained on the Pile and PushShift.io Reddit datasets using MHA and ReLU activation."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R796199> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/C15002> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R796199> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Resource> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R796199> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "OPT"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R796200> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/description> "The ReLU (Rectified Linear Unit) activation function is defined as: ReLU(x) = max(0, x). This means that the function outputs the input value x if it is positive, and 0 if it is negative. ReLU is popular due to its simplicity and effectiveness in training deep neural networks, as it helps mitigate the vanishing gradient problem by allowing gradients to flow when the input is positive."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R796200> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/C15002> .
