<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R8357> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/P184241> "The model of this paper represents scientific claims as first-class RDF assertions with provenance metadata, enabling basic computational querying of simple facts. However, it lacks advanced claim semantics (e.g., context or uncertainty) and does not model evidence such as experimental results or datasets as first-class entities, instead linking to them only via provenance. Consequently, validating complex scientific claims computationally requires integration with external evidence models.It does NOT enable computational validation"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/R8357> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/P184243> "1) RDF and Named Graphs - core representation format\n2) SWAN (Semantic Web Applications in Neuromedicine) ontologies:\na) Scientific Discourse ontology\nb) Provenance, Annotation and Versioning (PAV) ontology\n\n3) SIOC (Semantically-Interlinked Online Communities) - for discourse representation\n4) FOAF (Friend of a Friend) - for representing people, organizations, and agents\n5) Semantic Web Publishing ontology - for attribution and digital signatures\n6) Linked Data principles - for resource identification and linking\n7) RDFa - mentioned as facilitating statement creation from HTML"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
