<http://orkg.org/orkg/resource/wikidata:Q899670> <http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/P7037> "Emissivity in remote sensing refers to the efficiency with which an object or surface emits thermal radiation or heat. It is a dimensionless value between 0 and 1, where 0 indicates a perfect thermal reflector (emitting no radiation), and 1 represents a perfect thermal emitter (emitting all incident radiation). Emissivity is an essential parameter in thermal infrared remote sensing and is used to determine the temperature of surfaces and objects based on their thermal radiation. Accurate knowledge of emissivity is crucial for various applications, such as land surface temperature estimation, industrial monitoring, and environmental studies."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
